Three things to notice:
  - The witness text is the LIVE text, character for character: `enter heap_ok alloc_ok free_ok
    new_rt MmMmMm`, then nothing. The child is a python interpreter with no Stellaris in it.
  - `SANITY 42` printed first, so this is the same entry order the shot uses (gate, then sequence).
  - The child never printed `SURVIVED rc=`. The process died inside the call, status 3.

Run twice this session under different ASLR bases (0x7ffbeb710000 earlier, 0x7ffc138c0000 here); the
in-DLL RVA set is identical both times. It is deterministic, not a race.

## (3) the panic is in OUR build's QuickJS, and it is a safety trap, not a crash

`member access within null pointer of type 'JSMallocBlockHeader' (aka 'struct JSMallocBlockHeader')`
is clang's UBSan wording. Zig links its own UBSan runtime (`lib/ubsan_rt.zig`), whose `typeMismatch`
prints exactly `"{s} null pointer of type {s}"` for kind `member_access`, then panics; and Zig's panic
handler aborts. `JSMallocBlockHeader` exists ONLY in `libs/quickjs/quickjs.c:270-280`, so the trip is
inside the vendored QuickJS, in code that our build compiled with safety checks on.

QuickJS 2025-09-13 (CONFIG_VERSION at quickjs.c) no longer uses the host allocator directly. It
bundles an arena: `struct JSRuntime` begins with `JSMallocContext malloc_ctx` (quickjs.c:317-320),
which holds its own `mf` + `malloc_state` and hands out blocks with an 8-byte header in front of every
user pointer:

    static inline JSMallocBlockHeader *js_rc(void *ptr) {          // quickjs.c:1492
        return container_of(ptr, JSMallocBlockHeader, user_data);  // quickjs.c:1494  ⇒ ptr − 8
    }

So `JS_NewRuntime2` (quickjs.c:2067) allocates the JSRuntime through OUR `heap_malloc` once, then
routes everything else through its own arena, calling us only for arenas and large blocks.

## (4) pinning the site offline — no symbols, no debug info, no process

The DLL has no symbol table and no `.debug_*` sections, so Zig's printer can only say `???`. Two
things in the image still answer "where", and `ubsan_site.py` uses them:

  * every UBSan check carries a `TypeMismatchData` record in `.rdata` holding the source file, line,
    column and the type-descriptor name — the same strings the panic printed;
  * `.pdata` is the x64 exception directory: `{BeginAddress, EndAddress}` per function, i.e. exact
    function boundaries without symbols.

Feeding the panic's innermost RVA (0xbd9b, absolute 0x18000bd9b at the usual image base) in:

    $ python3 scripts/scratch/2026-10_abortexit/ubsan_site.py zig-out/bin/stellaris_quickjs.dll func 0x18000bd9b
    [ubsan_site] function holding 0x18000bd9b: rva 0xbd70..0xbdf3 (131 bytes)
      /var/lofibass_ssd/code/zig/Neo-Stellaris/libs/quickjs/quickjs.c:1494:12 member_access type='JSMallocBlockHeader' (aka 'struct JSMallocBlockHeader') record_rva=0x5aa430 loaded_at=0x18000bd8c

and objdump agrees at the byte level — the `lea rcx,[rip+0x59e69d]` at 0x18000bd8c computes 0x1805aa430,
the record for **quickjs.c:1494:12, i.e. `js_rc`'s `container_of`**, and the panic address 0xbd9b is
that call's return address minus one (Zig prints `ip−1` for a frame; the `call` occupies
0x18000bd97..0x18000bd9c). `add rcx,0xfffffffffffffff8` a few bytes later is the `ptr − 8`.

The third in-DLL frame (0x18001ebde → rva 0x1ebb0..0x1eda4) references records at
**quickjs.c:3433:15 and 3434:8**, both on type `JSString` plus the unnamed union at quickjs.c:595.
Reading those lines names the function without any symbol table:

    static JSAtom __JS_NewAtomInit(JSRuntime *rt, const char *str, int len, int atom_type)  // 3427
    { p = js_alloc_string_rt(rt, len, 0); if (!p) return JS_ATOM_NULL;
      memcpy(p->u.str8, str, len); p->u.str8[len] = '\0'; ... }                             // 3433-3434

`__JS_NewAtomInit` is exactly what `JS_InitAtoms` (quickjs.c:3169) runs ~504 times, and `JS_InitAtoms`
is the first thing `JS_NewRuntime2` does after `js_malloc_init` — i.e. between allocation #3 and a
hypothetical #4, which is where §42 parked the death.

Mechanically, `js_rc` is reached with the header pointer equal to zero, which for `container_of(ptr,…)`
means the user pointer it was handed was 8 — the value `__js_malloc` produces for `b->user_data` when
its block header `b` is NULL (quickjs.c:1588, 1618). The `if (!p)` guard in `__JS_NewAtomInit` cannot
catch that: 8 is not NULL. That is the shape of the defect; the reason `b` comes back NULL on Windows
and not on Linux is not yet known.

## (5) the same sequence natively, for contrast

The Linux `c2_diag_tests` step (build.zig:123) runs the SAME `c2_diag_sequence` against the same
vendored QuickJS and completes: `new_rt MmMmMmMmMmMmMmMmrt=0x2235f28`, rc=0, 8 matched allocation
pairs and a runtime pointer. So this is not "QuickJS's arena is broken everywhere" — it is this build,
on this platform. build.zig's own comment ("QuickJS's bundled allocator misbehaves under Zig's musl
static libc") records that the allocator path is already target-sensitive; the tests link glibc
dynamically for that reason.

## (6) reading — what this retroactively changes

  1. **§36's fork was the wrong question.** "Heap/environment side vs engine side" both assume the
     host was alive and executing our sequence while it died. The artifact kills its own host in a
     bare python child. Nothing about Stellaris is required to reproduce the death.
  2. **§42's "6.9 s zero-allocation stretch inside QuickJS's own code" is void.** §44's memory series
     releases monotonically from the first sample (8,179,876 K private → 2,156 K), which is teardown,
     and §44's gle=5-at-80 ms says the thread was already unsuspendable. There was no long computation.
  3. **The escalation §42 asked for is not needed.** "Seeing into that gap requires hooking QuickJS =
     升级授权" was based on reading the gap as QuickJS-executes-without-allocating. The gap does not
     exist, and the site is now pinned from the file copy with read-only tooling. No capability
     escalation was performed or is required for the next step.
  4. **`rc = 3` decoded as `bucket=intrinsic name=Date` is retracted again, now with a mechanism.**
     §38.5 retracted it as unproven; here it is explained: 3 is abort()'s process exit status, and the
     thread reported it because a terminated thread reports the process status.
  5. **C2 is still blocked, but the blocker moved.** It is no longer "the host dies when our init
     sequence runs" — it is "our build's QuickJS trips a null-header member access in its own atom-init
     string allocation, aborts, and that abort is what the host's exit status 3 has been reporting
     since §31". The target of the next step is our artifact, and the loop is
     `edit → zig build → scp under a new name → python C:\temp\dll_abort_repro.py` with no game, no
     dispatch and no shot authorization needed.
  6. **A corollary worth naming:** the deployed DLL is a Debug build (build.zig's
     `standardOptimizeOption(.{})` default), so Zig/clang safety checks are compiled into the code that
     runs inside the game, and a safety trip is a host process kill. That is a property of every shot
     so far, not of this defect alone.

Honest boundaries:
  - The repro involves NO game process, NO injection and NO dispatch; the child python is the only
    process that died.
  - "The user pointer was 8" is derived from what the emitted check tests (`ptr − 8 == 0`) plus the
    source shape; the faulting value itself was never observed. The disassembled guard at 0x18000bd86
    has an ambiguous shape (`xor eax,eax; test al,0x1; jne` ⇒ the handler call looks unconditional in
    -O0 codegen), so I am NOT claiming clang proved nullness at compile time.
  - Frame attribution uses `.pdata` ranges and UBSan record references, not symbol names; the
    `__JS_NewAtomInit` identification rests on its two records being at lines 3433/3434, which is
    strong but is a source-line match rather than a link-time name. The frame between the trap and
    `__JS_NewAtomInit` (RVA 0xeede3 — where `js_alloc_string_rt`/`js_malloc_rt` would sit) has no
    `.pdata` entry covering it, and that parse yields only 3,042 function entries for a 0x4c8b86-byte
    `.text`, so absence of an entry says nothing about the code; the middle of the chain is inferred.
  - WHY the arena's block header comes back NULL on Windows and not on Linux is open. Candidates the
    next step should rank, none of them tested here: our `AllocHeader` vs the alignment QuickJS's arena
    assumes; `heap_malloc`'s `size == 0` → NULL (glibc returns a unique pointer); `malloc_limit`/
    `malloc_size` arithmetic across the `JSMallocState` we own vs the one `JS_NewRuntime2` seeds.
  - One build, one artifact, two runs of the same child. The determinism claim covers those two runs.

## (7) read-only self-attestation

    - no hook/Interceptor/patchCode/writeByteArray/Memory.protect; no new capability; the DLL bytes are
      byte-identical to §42's and were not modified.
    - every observation here is either (a) a file copy of the DLL read offline, or (b) a child python
      that this script spawned, loaded the DLL into itself, and died.
    - no game process was involved; no init dispatch was made (the §44 单发射 stays spent); the Windows
      host ran only `python` and `Get-FileHash`.
